 you can spray it on and  watch it move into the plant  and we looked at various factors to see  if any factors might aid in the uptake  of the urea that was labeled  and we looked at  surfactants  we looked at you know time of day  we looked at the ph as a solution we  looked at plants that were stressed or  not because a lot of the companies that  sell these products say well you know  you didn't put them on at the right time  or didn't have the right surfactant and  stuff like that this is our control  we were getting his you know our  standard treatment just mixing it with  water and spraying on was giving us  almost 50 percent of the nitrogen being  absorbed almost anything else we do  either didn't make a difference or  actually  reduce the uptake  so  i don't think there's anything magic if  you want to use it about half of it goes  on  we did the same thing  looking at a potassium analog in this  case we looked at rubidium which is a  periodic chart it's the same valence as  potassium behaves the same way  sprayed to rubidium on looked at uptake  again almost the same thing our standard  treatment was pretty much as good as we  could get  maybe putting on early in the morning  was a little bit better but for the most  part it didn't make a difference  the problem with these foliar  applications though is that even though  we get 50 uptake you can only put on  about five pounds of actual urea per  acre at any one time or else you get  toxicity to the leaf if the plant's only  taking up 50 of that  that's two and a half pounds and truth  of the matter is not all that you spray  on hits the plant some of it just goes  off into the air it never really touches  a plant so  in actuality you're probably only really  getting into the plant one or one and a  half pounds of actual nitrogen with  every time you spray  so it's not a very efficient way of  applying nitrogen you almost have to  spray every day to get give the plant  the nitrogen it needs from foliar  applications and our data show that it  doesn't make any difference anyway when  it's sprayed during the growing season  the only time we're seeing the  difference is when it's sprayed late in  the fall  to contribute to those nitrogen reserves  so to wrap everything up  pre-plan amendments are important  bases on soil test recommendations from  a reputable lab  use leaf analysis to fine-tune your  fertilization and establish plantings  and generally not always but generally  restrict nitrogen applications to the  spring  although evidence exists of building  reserve nitrogen late fall can be  helpful if carbohydrate status is  adequate  so  all right  thank you thank you marvin does anybody  have any questions and i apologize if  there's some problem with the audio for  some people um it sounded really good to  me but that doesn't mean that everyone  else is having good reception and i  certainly would appreciate feedback  about how the quality of the  presentation  and  so this is your opportunity to ask  marvin some questions about the  the presentation does anybody have any  questions  if not i'm going to move into the final  oh hold on  stephanie has a question stephanie  would you i think you have a microphone  now if you have one or you could type  the question in the chat box  okay i'm not hearing from stephanie you  might want to  type right down at the bottom of the  chat box stephanie or if you have a  microphone i you are unable to to ask  the question right now  so molly just asked a question about  were the last comments  about foliar feeding for strawberries or  raspberries the data i showed you an  uptake was for raspberries  the data i showed you on the actual  yield response for fall foliar  fertilization was for strawberries but  strawberries and raspberries  are fairly close related and most of the  time when people do these studies they  find that strawberries and raspberries  generally spawn the same way  so i wouldn't be surprised  if we did those same studies that we did  on strawberries with fall foliar  applications we did that to  raspberries whether we would see the  same response ylang chung here in the  department has done those  studies on grapes and apples and he's  found the exact same thing that late  fall  foliary applications increase nitrogen  reserves and improve yields the  following year provided that  carbohydrate levels are not too low  i don't see why raspberries would be any  different  um marvin there was a question from dan  welch about differences in nutrients for  day neutral strawberries  okay yeah so day neutral strawberries  are quite different than the regular  perennial strawberries they have very  shallow root systems  they allocate only about  10 of their growth to roots and that's  pretty low compared to like a raspberry  where it's 50 or more so these really  shallow roots they don't go out very far  so with they neutral strawberries you  really do have to  spoon feed those nitrogen almost all  season long  and we recommend  about one pound  of actual nitrogen a day for day neutral  strawberries during the growing season  now you don't have to fertilize them  every day unless you're set up to do  that you can put a pound of nitrogen  through the irrigation system  once a day but we don't recommend going  more than about seven days before you do  apply  that nitrogen so if you're gonna do it  weekly  you know seven pounds every week would  be equivalent one pound a day but they  need that much to get them through and  if you add that up over the entire  season they're using more nitrogen than  a regular strawberry it probably  shouldn't say they're using more but  they just  need a lot of nitrogen around because  their roots aren't really very good at  sequestering what's there so you spoon  feed them  and uh about one pound a day the other  nutrients potassium  phosphorus and all  that's the same you know if you put  enough in the ground before you plant  then you don't have to worry about that  and they're they're grown mostly as  annuals anyway so that's not a big deal  with daniel's but the nitrogen is  and we don't have to worry for most part  about  fall folder applications because most  people are not overwintering their day  neutrals anyway but if they would it  would probably have the same response as  a june bearing  craig thank you very much for answering  that one question  these series have all been recorded and  this one will also be available it was  recorded today  and we will open them up to the general  public shortly  so you can  you know you will be able to get them or  and recommend them to growers if you  would like to do that  i want to thank  dr pritz for taking the time today and  earlier in the session and also greg  loeb and courtney weber because they  were the other speakers it was a little  bit different presentation style and i  really appreciate them  uh  you know taking the time to learn how to  do it and everything i also want to  thank all of you for joining us it  really wouldn't be any good if we didn't  have anybody listening and asking  questions  and i do want to ask if you folks  wouldn't mind just taking one quick exit  poll  and i'll deliver this now  just give us a little feedback  this is an um multiple answer so you'll  just  read the questions as they're numbered  and then answer them in the boxes just  click on the box you don't have to hit a  submit or anything  and we will be able to have that data  to help us evaluate the sessions and  then also  maybe move forward with some other  webcast training  and then unless you have any other  questions for marvin after this poll is  done  that's it thank you very much  pretty much everybody that i expected  to weigh in has weighed in and i  appreciate that  and if anything else to add please go  ahead otherwise have a great day  you


